even before the pandemic the majority of  americans lived  paycheck to paycheck and when finances  are so tight it can be hard to think  about how you can save  extra money but today we're teaming up  with  chime to talk about getting on better  financial ground  and here to help us is money expert and  award-winning author  melissa leong  hi melissa i am so excited to be  speaking  about this with you and i'm going to  start off with this question  which is what is the first thing  that you should do if you're struggling  to save  money so  when you are feeling anxious or stressed  out about something it is  completely understandable to want to  just look away  from the dark and scary place you're  like i don't want to know  but i promise if you just turn the  lights on in your financial house and  take a look  that action is very powerful you need to  understand what's happening before you  can figure out  what you're going to do about it and so  i would download  print out all of my bank statements my  financial statements from the last say  three months  and see how much money has been coming  in and how much money is going out and  to where  you want to get an idea of your income  your expenses  categorize them i i'm old school so i do  it with a paper and a pen but you can do  with apps you can do with a spreadsheet  you're essentially creating a budget and  this will give you a better sense of  your situation  as you move forward i'm old school too  because i was picturing multiple color  highlighters  on that paper so what if someone decides  to  face it and then what they find is even  worse  than what they were trying to run away  from in the first place  so if you're brave enough to face it  first of all high five that's an  incredible feat for something that is  often very stressful for people  but once you do if you find that you are  in a situation where you don't have  enough money to pay for more things  beyond your essential needs  then you really only have two main  options make more money  or cut back so let's talk about cutting  back you know you've already created  this wonderful color-coded spreadsheet  with  your highlighters and your sticky notes  divide them into needs and once and then  go line by line  and figure out what you can cut and what  you can trim now when it comes to your  needs you can negotiate with your  service providers call them i just did  this with my insurance company i called  them i said it's the pandemic i'm not  commuting  and they lowered my premiums now when it  comes to your discretionary expenses  your wants  you can't meet you can't leave nothing  for fun right because  that is a recipe for a disaster but you  can agree have a conversation with  yourself and say you know what  for the next specific period of time i'm  just going to make some sacrifices  maybe that looks like cancelling or  pausing some subscriptions or  memberships maybe that looks like  a month of no spending so  only on essentials where you would spend  or maybe no spend mondays  wednesdays and fridays but whatever it  is that you cut you want to make sure  that you allocate that amount  to savings otherwise you'll just end up  spending it  on other things okay so your latest  paycheck comes in  what do you tackle first  well it can often feel like the money  comes in and then just  it's gone right especially if a lot of  your bills are automated  but again you've done this this budget  this spreadsheet  and you have a better idea of where  everything is going um  if your bills are not automated you want  to list them  and write them when they are due so that  you are not  well it sucks to waste money on late  fees for  missed payments right and so after you  take care of your necessities  the thing to focus on there are two  things debt repayment  and savings so when it comes to savings  no amount is too  little savings is a habit and so you  want to flex that muscle as much as  possible even if it's starting with a  small amount  and then setting a calendar reminder say  three months from now to bump that up  you want to download your bank app your  financial app to your phone so that it's  there you can be more mindful  of the inflow and outflow of your money  and you can also  try a tool like chime it's an  award-winning finance app and a debit  card with no service fees  you turn on your notifications and you  will get a notification  with every transaction and regular  balance updates  it's an easy way to help you stick to  your budget  what is your favorite money saving hacks  may i ask  so i am a busy sleep deprived  mom i cannot be relied upon to make  money smart choices  all the time and so i automate my  savings as much as possible  so actually chimes mission is to give  americans peace of mind  and they allow its members to  automatically transfer 10  of every direct deposit of 500 or more  into an optional savings account that is  an easy way to save money for your goals  it is  essentially putting your savings on  autopilot so when you put your savings  on autopilot what you also want to do  is you want to make sure that you're  having money come out say  twice a month on payday rather than  waiting until the end of the month  to see what is left waiting until the  end of the month  is like my husband asking me to save him  some chips  i'll just eat it all and then there'll  be no more  unless i set aside a handful of chips  for him before i start eating  so this is where you'll want to have  multiple streams of money coming out to  various goals so rainy day fund um  homeownership maybe retirement when it  comes to retirement if you have  a retirement plan with your employer  you'll want to maximize that especially  if they  match some of your or all of your  contributions that's like free money  and finally um we talked a little bit  about this how difficult it can be to  get motivated when it comes to money  and so this is the time where people  need to lean on their family and friends  you know according to research people  who had peer support actually saved  more money than those without it and so  find  well chosen people i mean not you know  the friends who peer pressure you to  spend money or encourage you to compete  but  people you can share your dreams with  you know someone you can say you don't  have to tell them how much you make or  how much debt you have but you can say  we're gonna check in every friday  together we're gonna do a fun money  challenge  and we'll talk about our goals you know  i have a group chat that i check in  um i check with them before i buy almost  anything actually and they're this  lovely non-judgmental  group who say mel you know this  virtual shoulder shake through the phone  you have other  goals that you want to save for you  probably don't need that do you  i think if we just talked more about  money with each other it would  empower us all i agree this is something  we cannot avoid  and that we need to figure out and i  think time  for the systems in which they're putting  into place to change people's lives and  i thank you  melissa leung for your incredible wise  wonderful sage  useful advice  [Music]  you
 

